- Looks as if many parts of this will have to be implemented:
    http://www.yardley.cc/atari/compendium/atari-compendium-appendb.htm
    http://www.yardley.cc/atari/compendium/atari-compendium-appendb2.htm
- Mode 7 of Pexec takes the program flags a program header carries, and tosemu
  acts on none of them, so it is mode 5 with an argument it ignores. Same for
  the flags in a program header itself.
- A child of Pexec mode 0 gets the whole of memory rather than a TPA carved
  out of what its parent left free, so it is handed more than TOS would give
  it. An application that Mallocs before it Mshrinks gets nothing, where on
  TOS it would get what the parent released. The modes that load a program
  into the caller's memory do carve it out, and do not have this.
- Only sixteen children of the asynchronous Pexec modes can be waited for at
  once, and Pwait3 ignores the resource usage it is handed, which tosemu has
  nothing to fill in.
- The BIOS device handles, -1 CON:, -2 AUX: and -3 PRN:, which GEMDOS accepts
  everywhere a handle is taken. Lattice's startup code calls Fforce(4, -2).
  tosemu has a console but no serial port or printer, so -1 is the one with an
  answer to give, and the other two keep reporting EIHNDL.
- Console output goes straight to the host rather than through handle 1, so
  Cconout and Cconws ignore an Fforce that redirected it.
- Ptermres, which has nowhere to stay resident in.
- remove_memory_area never advances its pointer, so it only ever finds the
  first area. reset_memory happens to always ask for that one.
- Objects an application draws itself - G_USERDEF, which a resource editor
  calls G_PROGDEF - are drawn as empty boxes. The object's ob_spec points at
  an APPLBLK holding the address of a routine in the machine's memory, and
  calling it is more than reaching it: what the routine is handed is a
  PARMBLK describing what to draw, and in that block is the address of the
  tree. That has to be the application's own tree rather than the copy
  aestree.c makes, because the application can only read its own, and it has
  to be built in the machine's memory in the machine's layout - both blocks
  are, and the routine walks them. So this needs the Supexec trampoline in
  emuvdi/gemoblib.c to construct a PARMBLK there, fill it from the host side
  of the seam, and hand back what the routine returns. lattice/EXAMPLES/WTEST
  has two of them and is the thing to test it against.
- Add a configuration item (or command line option) to select from six 
  different graphics modes:
    1. 320x200, 4bpp (Atari ST)
    2. 640x200, 2bpp (Atari ST)
    3. 640x400, 1bpp (Atari ST)
    4. 1280x960, 1bpp (Atari TT)
    5. 640x480, 18bpp (Atari Falcon)
    6. Native screen resolution split by scaling factor, 24bpp (Best possible)
- Add a configuration item (or command line option) to select the memory size 
  from what the various Atari models (ST, TT and Falcon) offered, and then the
  maximum possible given the restrictions of the APIs.